-- The PRECEDENCE sibling of `usewild_ctor_overlay_oracle/`.  That fixture has ONE
-- import-visible module publishing the type in question, so it cannot see which SPELLING
-- of import outranks which.  This one has TWO, and the whole fixture is the question the
-- other cannot ask: when a WILDCARD clause and an EXPLICIT MEMBER LIST both put a type
-- spelled `Col` in scope, which declaration does a constructor occurrence mean?
--
-- 🚨 WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS.  `importedCtorTypeDecls` was a single `flatMap` over the
-- unit's decls in SOURCE ORDER, and that output order IS the precedence (the oracle's
-- `oracleMap` is `omFromPairs (reverseL …)`, i.e. first-wins).  While only `UseGroup`
-- contributed, every row was one precisely-named decl.  The `UseWild` arm made a wildcard
-- contribute the named module's WHOLE published set — so a wildcard clause written ABOVE
-- an explicit member list outranked it, and "you imported everything and this happened to
-- be in there" beat "you named this type".  The overlay is now PARTITIONED: named rows and
-- wildcard rows are separate halves, ordered per consumer (see the partition note at
-- `importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins`, compiler/types/typecheck.mdk).
--
-- THE CLAUSE ORDER HERE IS LOAD-BEARING: the wildcard is written FIRST, which is the
-- broken arm.  Do not "tidy" the imports into alphabetical order — that silently converts
-- this fixture into the arm that was always green.
--
-- EXPECTED RESULT, DERIVED BY HAND BEFORE ANY RUN (this corpus has NO regeneration
-- script; `oracle.json` is hand-written and reviewed precisely so a wrong engine answer
-- cannot become the permanent expected output):
--
--   `f` carries no signature, so its argument type is inferred from the `Red` pattern.
--   `Red` is bound by the member list and belongs to `aacol.Col`, whose constructors are
--   `Red` and `Blue`.  `Green`/`Purple` are constructors of a DIFFERENT type that merely
--   shares the spelling `Col`.  The single-arm match therefore omits exactly one case,
--   `Blue`, and that is the only diagnostic owed anywhere in this graph.  `main` applies
--   `f` only to `Red`, so nothing is owed at runtime.
--
-- MEASURED, on a binary cold-built from sprint/ctor-identity @ af6e0414 (the `UseWild`
-- arm, before this partition): this exact graph reported
-- `non-exhaustive match of 'Col'. Missing case: 'Green'` — a constructor of `zzcol.Col`,
-- a type the scrutinee is not — while the REAL missing case `Blue` went unreported and
-- the program died at runtime on the case nobody warned about.  Swapping the two import
-- lines reported `Blue` correctly.  Base `c7c879b7` reported `Blue` in BOTH orders.
import zzcol.*
import aacol.{Red, Blue, aval}

f x = match x
  Red => 1

main : <IO> Unit
main = println (f Red + aval)
